WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:11.780
The cubical represents a tyranny that confines
your imagination and thoughts are confined

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.260
to a small physical area.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.260
Imagine pretty much every software engineer
or finance person being able to disconnect

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:25.160
from their desk and look at holographic monitors
on a beach and doing their work from there.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.390
that’s not going to be science fiction
- it’s the modern office place.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.360
Silicon Valley is all about building the future.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.780
A startup called Meta thinks it’s getting
there first, thanks to a big bet it’s made

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.280
on Augmented Reality.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.360
How are you?

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.740
Good, Thank you. &nbsp;Welcome to Meta.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.700
Thank you.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.780
Call it a 360 degree office where you can
spatialize your thoughts as part of your workflow

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.980
for education, architecture, design, engineering,
etc.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.250
People often mix up Augmented Reality with Virtual
Reality.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.930
VR totally blocks your ability to see or
hear the real world.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:08.710
AR overlays holograms onto what you already
see.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:14.650
Meta has tried to make its version of the
workspace feel familiar.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.049
You grab the hologram, instead of using a
controller or a mouse and your brain already

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:20.040
knows how to do it.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.100
In other words, we’ve designed an operating
system that

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.580
humanity has always known how to use.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.560
So you can see this eyeball, which is, by
the why, photo-realistic.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.720
You can see my hand is including the eyeball
and now the eyeball is including my hand.

00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:33.729
Right.

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:34.729
See those two circles?

00:01:34.729 --> 00:01:38.259
They get small and then they turn into this
glowing white ball and then I can move my

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:39.619
hand around super naturally.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:42.979
I can do this with two hands and I can rotate
the thing, I can stretch it.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:47.780
I can throw it back into the shelving system
and that’s all you have to learn to become

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.909
a modern worker.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:53.700
And to prove this, the employees at Meta have
started to get rid of their computer monitors

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.680
-- trading them in for Meta’s augmented
reality headsets.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.740
Meron thinks it will be less than a decade
before we'll all be wearing strips of glass

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.640
that can project holograms.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:13.900
 

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.600
In the early 80s, everybody had computers
on their desktops but nobody was using them

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.200
because they had a lot of work to do.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.080
So they were still using typewriters.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.700
At some point the CEO took all of the typewriters
away and everyone was forced to use the computers

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:32.840
so it’s very exciting to see a new generation
of technology, a new paradigm.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.460
I consider us like pioneers in the holographic
wild.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.320
I’m, you know, pulling up my browser with
my hands and I’m sending out emails to my

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.520
colleagues and just kind of really acclimating
to the new environment.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:03:00.340
 

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:05.560
Now you’re putting on my computer essentially.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.290
Our digital lives live on our phones they
have all of our pictures and notes and things

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:16.240
- so why don’t you write yourself a little
sticky note.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.049
Go ahead and take your first right over the
sticky note and close your fist.

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:21.540
And now….

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.960
What?!

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:30.060
Meta’s own transition to Augmented Reality
has run into plenty of unexpected problems

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.829
and it’s still gonna be a while before you’ll
start to see these devices in YOUR office.

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:40.349
But I think it’s a future worth waiting
for.

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:45.730
If we could see these holograms between us,
we will have been able to share our work with

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:51.160
one another more naturally, more efficiently
and more productively than ever before.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.500
Humanity will have evolved slightly.

